Screen mirroring plugin

Mirrors the screen and audio of an Android phone or tablet in a window on the
desktop and allows controlling it with mouse and keyboard. The heavy lifting is
done by scrcpy (https://github.com/Genymobile/scrcpy), which is started over
Android 11+ wireless debugging. Audio forwarding needs Android 11 or newer.
Nothing has to be installed on the device besides KDE Connect.

Requirements on the desktop: scrcpy and adb (Android platform tools). On
Windows the adb.exe that comes with the scrcpy release is used automatically.

Usage
1. Enable the plugin for the device.
2. On the device: Settings > Developer options > Wireless debugging, enable it,
   tap "Pair device with pairing code" and enter the code in the plugin
   settings. This is needed only once.
3. Start mirroring from the device page or the Plasma applet. Wireless
   debugging has to be enabled on the device for that; the "Wireless
   debugging" quick settings tile (Developer options > Quick settings
   developer tiles) makes this one tap.

Security model
adb access is equivalent to a shell on the device, so the plugin tries to keep
it as narrow as possible:

- Only TLS based wireless debugging (Android 11+) with explicit pairing is
  used. The legacy unauthenticated "adb tcpip 5555" mode is never enabled.
- The plugin is disabled by default and only offered for phones and tablets.
- Each session uses its own adb server on a random port, bound to 127.0.0.1,
  that is killed when the session ends. It is independent of any adb server
  used by development tools.
- On Linux and macOS every device gets its own adb key, stored in
  ~/.local/share/kdeconnect/screenmirror/<device id>/.android with owner-only
  permissions. Forgetting the pairing deletes the key. On Windows adb always
  uses the key in the user profile.
- The device is only contacted at the IP address of the authenticated KDE
  Connect link. mDNS announcements from other addresses are ignored, and mDNS
  auto-connect is disabled. The device certificate is pinned by adb during
  pairing, so a spoofed announcement can not make adb talk to another device.
- The pairing code is passed to adb on stdin, so it does not show up in the
  process list.
- By default wireless debugging is turned off on the device when the session
  ends (settings put global adb_wifi_enabled 0), so it is only reachable while
  a session is running. This can be disabled in the plugin settings.
- Control of the device can be disabled to get a view-only mirror.

Remaining risks: while a session is running, other local users on the same
computer can reach the loopback adb server. Android revokes debugging
authorizations that are unused for 7 days unless "Disable adb authorization
timeout" is enabled in the developer options; keep it disabled.
